<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n6581" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀναίδεια</span></b> , 
			Ep.
		 and 
			Ion.
		
		<b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀναιδείη</span></b> ; 
			Att.
		 also <b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀναιδείᾱ</span></b> 
		<i><span class="en">
			Ar.
			<u>Fr.</u>
			226
		</span></i>, poet. <b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀναιδία</span></b> 
			Hdn.Gr.<i><span class="en">
			2.453
		</span></i>:—<div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>shamelessness,</i>
			
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀναιδείην ἐπιειμένε</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Il.
					1.149
				</span></i>
			; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀναιδείης ἐπιβῆναι</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Od.
					22.424
				</span></i>
			; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἡ γαστὴρ φρένας παρήγαγεν εἰς
					ἀναιδείην</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Archil.
					78
				</span></i>
			; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀναιδείῃ διαχρεώμενοι</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Hdt.
					7.210
				</span></i>
			, cf. <i><span class="en">
				6.129
			</span></i>; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀναιδείας πλέα</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					S.
					<u>El.</u>
					607
				</span></i>
			; <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">μετ᾽ ἀναιδείας,</span>
			
				=
				<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀναιδῶς</span>
			, <i><span class="en">
				Pl.
				<u>Phdr.</u>
				254d
			</span></i>; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">εἰς τοῦθ᾽ ἧκεν ἀναιδείας</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					D.
					18.22
				</span></i>
			. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>II.</b> in the
			Areopagus, <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">λίθος ἀναιδείας</span> was
			the stone of <i>unforgivingness,</i> on which stood an accuser who
			demanded the full penalty of the law against one accused of homicide
			(v. 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">αἰδέομαι</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					11.3
				</span></i>
			), <i><span class="en">
				Paus.
				1.28.5
			</span></i>; cf. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὕβρις.</span></div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
